# **Normalise line endings to LF everywhere.** This solves two problems:
# 1) Without normalisation, a group with a mix of Windows and Mac/Linux
#    machines eventually commits a file with the other platform's line endings
#    and git reports every single line of it as changed -- which buries the
#    one line that actually changed and makes the diff useless as evidence of
#    who did what.
# 2) Pinning eol=lf stops Windows git printing a screenful of "LF will be
#    replaced by CRLF" warnings the first time it looks at the files.
# Every tool used here (Quartus, ModelSim, Verilator, Python, any modern
# editor) is happy with LF files on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly text, so diffs and merges work properly:
*.v      text eol=lf
*.sv     text eol=lf
*.sdc    text eol=lf
*.qpf    text eol=lf
*.qsf    text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.py     text eol=lf
# A .sh file with CRLF endings also fails to run on Linux or macOS with
# "bad interpreter: ...^M":
*.sh     text eol=lf

# Explicitly binary, so git never tries to merge or diff them:
*.sof    binary
*.pof    binary
*.qar    binary
*.fst    binary
*.png    binary
*.pdf    binary

# A .vcd waveform is actually ASCII text, so it is not marked binary -- but a
# diff of one is thousands of unreadable value-change lines, so turn diffs off.
*.vcd    -diff
